window.SAT_DATA = window.SAT_DATA || {};
(window.SAT_DATA.rwBank = window.SAT_DATA.rwBank || []).push(
{"id": "rw-cid-b1", "domain": "information", "skill": "central-ideas", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Neuroscientist Dana Ellsworth and colleagues tracked the neural signatures of newly formed memories across a night of sleep. Conventional accounts hold that sleep simply stabilizes memories, insulating them from decay. Ellsworth's recordings suggest a more active process: during deep sleep, the hippocampus repeatedly replayed the day's episodes to the cortex, but the replayed patterns were not faithful copies. Instead, shared features across episodes were amplified while idiosyncratic details faded, so that sleepers awoke able to state a general rule they had never been taught while forgetting the specific examples from which it was distilled."}, "stem": "Which choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": [{"text": "Sleep is important for both the acquisition of new skills and the long-term retention of memories.", "why": "Too vague to be the main idea, and it echoes the very view the text revises; the passage's point is that sleep changes memories, not merely that it retains them.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "During deep sleep, the hippocampus repeatedly replays the previous day's episodes to the cortex.", "why": "This is an accurate mechanistic detail, but it is a step in Ellsworth's account rather than the overall claim, which concerns what that replay accomplishes.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "Rather than merely safeguarding memories, sleep actively reworks them, distilling general regularities from particular experiences.", "why": "Captures the text's contrast between the 'conventional' stabilizing view and Ellsworth's 'more active process,' in which shared features are amplified and details fade so a general rule emerges."}, {"text": "Sleep strengthens the vivid, idiosyncratic details of an experience while allowing the broader patterns that connect experiences to fade.", "why": "Reverses the text: the passage states that shared features were amplified while idiosyncratic details faded, the opposite of this claim.", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-cid-b2", "domain": "information", "skill": "central-ideas", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "The rebbelib and mattang of the Marshall Islands are lattices of palm ribs and cowrie shells that Western observers long catalogued as nautical maps. Yet navigators did not carry these frames aboard their canoes. The curved ribs encode not coastlines but the refraction and interference patterns of ocean swells as they bend around islands—knowledge a navigator internalized on land and then read directly from the motion of the hull at sea. The artifact, in other words, was a device for training perception rather than a chart to be consulted during the voyage itself."}, "stem": "Based on the text, which choice best describes the Marshall Islands stick charts?", "choices": [{"text": "They were instruments for teaching navigators to perceive the behavior of ocean swells, not maps consulted while sailing.", "why": "Matches the text's closing distinction: the charts were 'a device for training perception rather than a chart to be consulted during the voyage,' knowledge internalized on land."}, {"text": "They were portable maps that Marshallese navigators consulted throughout their ocean voyages to locate distant islands.", "why": "Reverses the text, which states that navigators 'did not carry these frames aboard their canoes.'", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "Their curved ribs represent the way ocean swells refract and interfere as they bend around islands.", "why": "A true supporting detail about what the ribs encode, but it describes one feature rather than characterizing the object's purpose, which is the focus of the text.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "They illustrate how frequently outside observers misinterpret objects produced by unfamiliar cultures.", "why": "Generalizes far beyond the passage, which discusses one misread cataloguing rather than a broad pattern of misinterpretation.", "tag": "too-broad"}], "answer": 0},
{"id": "rw-cid-b3", "domain": "information", "skill": "central-ideas", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Written scripts are at most a few thousand years old—far too recent for any brain region to have evolved to process them. Studying newly literate adults, neuroscientist Priya Raghavan found that learning to read did not recruit fresh cortical territory. Instead, a patch of visual cortex that in nonreaders responds to faces and objects was gradually reassigned to recognizing letters, its responses to faces migrating toward the opposite hemisphere. Literacy, she concludes, does not add a module to the brain so much as it competes with existing functions for room in circuitry that predates writing entirely."}, "stem": "Which choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": [{"text": "The human brain retains a remarkable capacity for change well into adulthood.", "why": "A generic statement about plasticity that omits the text's specific claim about how reading reuses pre-existing visual circuitry.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "In newly literate adults, the visual cortex's responses to faces shift toward the opposite hemisphere.", "why": "An accurate detail from Raghavan's findings, but it reports one observation rather than the passage's central conclusion about repurposing.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "Because reading competes with face recognition for cortical space, people who learn to read become worse at recognizing faces.", "why": "The text describes face responses migrating, not declining; it never claims that face-recognition ability deteriorates.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Learning to read does not create new brain tissue but repurposes visual circuitry evolved for other tasks, displacing some of its original functions.", "why": "Captures Raghavan's conclusion that literacy 'competes with existing functions for room in circuitry that predates writing,' reassigning a face- and object-processing region to letters."}], "answer": 3},
{"id": "rw-cid-b4", "domain": "information", "skill": "central-ideas", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "The knotted-cord records called khipu, kept by administrators of the Inca state, have long been read as accounting devices: the position and type of each knot denote quantities in a decimal system. But numbers alone cannot explain every khipu. On some cords the knots do not resolve into tidy sums, and the color, ply direction, and fiber of the cords vary in ways that appear to carry meaning of their own. Analyzing a matched pair of khipu from one village, researcher Sabine Ortiz argues that such features may have recorded names and social categories—suggesting, though not yet proving, that the medium could encode language as well as arithmetic."}, "stem": "Which choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": [{"text": "In a matched pair of khipu from a single village, the color and ply of the cords appear to record people's names.", "why": "Reports the specific case Ortiz examined rather than the broader claim the text builds from it about what khipu could encode.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "Although khipu were long understood as purely numerical records, features of some cords suggest they may also have encoded non-numerical information such as language.", "why": "Captures the text's arc from the accounting interpretation to Ortiz's hedged proposal that khipu 'could encode language as well as arithmetic,' with the qualifier 'not yet proving' preserved."}, {"text": "Analysis has now confirmed that khipu recorded only quantities and never conveyed linguistic information.", "why": "Reverses the passage, which argues the opposite—that khipu may have carried linguistic meaning—and overstates certainty the text explicitly withholds.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "Scholars are continually revising their understanding of how early societies stored and transmitted information.", "why": "A generic observation that could describe countless studies; it omits the specific claim about what khipu may have recorded.", "tag": "too-broad"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-cid-b5", "domain": "information", "skill": "central-ideas", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Placebo analgesia is often dismissed as mere reporting bias: patients who expect relief simply say they hurt less. Recording from the spinal cord as well as the brain, neuroscientist Tomas Lindqvist and colleagues challenged that view. When participants were led to expect a soothing cream, the activity evoked by a painful stimulus was dampened not only in cortical regions but in the dorsal horn of the spinal cord, the first relay for pain signals entering the nervous system. Expectation, the team concluded, reaches down to reshape the sensory signal at its point of entry, before that signal is ever consciously appraised."}, "stem": "Which choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": [{"text": "A person's beliefs and expectations can exert a powerful influence over their bodily experiences.", "why": "True but far too general; it omits the text's specific finding about where in the nervous system expectation acts.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "Placebo analgesia arises entirely from reporting bias, as skeptics of the phenomenon have long maintained.", "why": "This is precisely the view Lindqvist's team 'challenged,' so it is the opposite of the text's conclusion.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "Expectation can lessen pain not merely by changing how it is reported but by dampening pain signals at their earliest entry into the nervous system.", "why": "Matches the conclusion that expectation reshapes the signal 'at its point of entry'—the dorsal horn—rather than only altering conscious report."}, {"text": "Painful stimuli evoke activity in the dorsal horn of the spinal cord, the first relay for signals entering the nervous system.", "why": "A definitional detail used to set up the finding, not the main point about what expectation does to that activity.", "tag": "too-specific"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-cid-b6", "domain": "information", "skill": "central-ideas", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Across the Australian interior, Aboriginal songlines trace the routes of ancestral beings through the landscape; to sing a songline in sequence is to recite the waterholes, ridges, and turnings of a path that may run for hundreds of miles. Anthropologists once treated these songs chiefly as mythology. More recent scholarship emphasizes their practical architecture: because each verse is anchored to a physical place encountered in order, a song functions at once as a navigational record and a mnemonic scaffold, allowing knowledge of water, food, and kinship obligations to be stored in, and retrieved from, the country itself."}, "stem": "Based on the text, which choice best describes songlines?", "choices": [{"text": "They serve not only as mythology but as practical systems that use features of the landscape to store and recall navigational and ecological knowledge.", "why": "Captures the text's pivot from treating songlines 'chiefly as mythology' to recognizing their 'practical architecture' as a navigational record and mnemonic scaffold tied to places."}, {"text": "They demonstrate that Aboriginal Australian cultures preserve an extensive body of oral tradition across many generations.", "why": "A broad generalization about oral tradition that leaves out the text's specific claim about how songlines encode practical knowledge.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "They tie each of their verses to a particular physical place, such as a waterhole or a ridge along a route.", "why": "An accurate detail about how songlines are organized, but it describes one structural feature rather than the passage's overall characterization of their function.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "Because they are fundamentally works of mythology, their descriptions of the landscape offer little practical value to travelers.", "why": "Reverses the text, which argues that songlines function as a navigational record and knowledge store precisely because verses map onto real places.", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 0},
{"id": "rw-cid-a1", "domain": "information", "skill": "central-ideas", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "A 1663 banquet still life long catalogued as the unassisted work of the Dutch painter Jacob Verhulst has recently been examined with infrared reflectography, which renders visible the underdrawing beneath a painting's surface. Art historian Marit Deckers reports that the composition's tableware was blocked in with confident, unbroken contour lines, whereas the floral passages were laid out in short, hatched strokes—a discrepancy she considers suggestive of a second hand, presumably a workshop assistant's. Deckers is careful to note, however, that seventeenth-century painters sometimes varied their preparatory technique to suit different pictorial problems, so the reflectography alone cannot rule out the possibility that Verhulst simply drew flowers and tableware differently."}, "stem": "Which choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": [{"text": "Infrared reflectography has established that a workshop assistant, not Verhulst, executed the floral passages of the still life.", "why": "Deckers calls the two-hands reading only 'suggestive,' and the final sentence explicitly allows that Verhulst may have drawn both passages himself; the text never assigns the flowers to an assistant as settled fact.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Infrared reflectography allows art historians to see the preparatory drawings that lie beneath the painted surface of a finished work.", "why": "This accurately defines the technique, but the definition is background for the attribution question the text is actually built around; it is a supporting detail, not the main idea.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "An examination of the underdrawing of a painting attributed to Verhulst has raised the possibility that an assistant contributed to it, though the evidence is compatible with another explanation.", "why": "The text moves from the reflectography findings ('suggestive of a second hand') to Deckers's explicit caution that the imaging 'cannot rule out' Verhulst varying his own technique—evidence raised, then deliberately qualified."}, {"text": "Deckers's examination of the still life confirms the traditional attribution by showing that Verhulst adapted his underdrawing technique to different subjects.", "why": "The text presents Verhulst's varying his technique as an unexcluded possibility, not a demonstrated conclusion; this choice converts Deckers's caution into a confirmation she never offers.", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-cid-a2", "domain": "information", "skill": "central-ideas", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Zircon crystals recovered from the Jack Hills of Western Australia—some nearly 4.4 billion years old—are the oldest known terrestrial materials, and their chemistry has unsettled the traditional picture of the Hadean eon as an interval of unrelieved molten desolation. Elevated ratios of oxygen-18 in many crystals imply that the magmas from which they grew incorporated material altered by liquid water at low temperatures, a scenario requiring stable crust, and perhaps oceans, far earlier than once assumed. Skeptics counter that such isotopic signatures can be reset when later fluids penetrate a crystal; proponents of a 'cool early Earth' have responded by restricting their analyses to crystal domains that imaging shows to be pristine."}, "stem": "Which choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": [{"text": "Chemical evidence from ancient zircons has challenged the assumption that the earliest Earth was uniformly molten, though the case depends on establishing that the crystals' signatures are original rather than products of later alteration.", "why": "The text states that the zircons' chemistry 'has unsettled the traditional picture' of the Hadean and then hinges the debate on alteration: skeptics invoke resetting by later fluids, and proponents answer by analyzing only pristine domains."}, {"text": "Because zircon crystals are easily altered by fluids, geologists have concluded that the Jack Hills zircons cannot provide trustworthy information about conditions during the Hadean eon.", "why": "This adopts the skeptics' objection as the field's verdict; the text presents alteration as a challenge that proponents address by restricting analyses to pristine crystal domains, not as grounds for dismissing the zircons.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "The Jack Hills of Western Australia contain the oldest known terrestrial materials, zircon crystals that formed nearly 4.4 billion years ago.", "why": "The zircons' record age is stated in the opening clause, but the text is organized around what their chemistry implies about early-Earth conditions and how secure that inference is; this is a detail, not the main idea.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "Studies of ancient minerals have transformed scientists' understanding of how the planets of the solar system originally formed.", "why": "The text concerns surface conditions on the early Earth specifically; nothing in it addresses planet formation or minerals beyond the Jack Hills zircons.", "tag": "too-broad"}], "answer": 0},
{"id": "rw-cid-a3", "domain": "information", "skill": "central-ideas", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "The melodies of the twelfth-century troubadours survive in chansonniers copied a century or more after the songs were composed, in a notation that specifies pitch while leaving duration almost entirely unrecorded. Early twentieth-century editors resolved this silence by applying the rhythmic modes of Parisian polyphony, producing transcriptions in regular triple meters that remain common in anthologies. Musicologist Hélène Carbonneau argues that these transcriptions owe less to medieval evidence than to their editors' expectations: no troubadour source prescribes modal rhythm, and the modes belong to a repertory removed from the troubadours in genre, region, and generation. Performance, she suggests, may instead have followed the flexible pacing of the poems' declamation—a possibility the notation neither confirms nor forecloses."}, "stem": "Which choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": [{"text": "Carbonneau has demonstrated that troubadour songs were performed in the flexible rhythms of spoken declamation rather than in regular meters.", "why": "Carbonneau only 'suggests' declamatory pacing as a possibility that the notation 'neither confirms nor forecloses'; this choice converts her hedged alternative into a demonstrated finding.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Because chansonniers were copied long after the troubadours' songs were composed, the melodies they preserve are too corrupted for their pitches to be trusted.", "why": "The text says the notation does specify pitch and faults only the rhythmic assumptions of modern editors; it never claims that the melodies' pitches are corrupted or unreliable.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Troubadour melodies survive in a notation that records pitch but gives almost no indication of the durations of notes.", "why": "True, but this is the background fact that sets up the argument; the text's main idea is Carbonneau's critique of how modern editors filled that rhythmic gap.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "Carbonneau contends that a standard way of transcribing troubadour melodies rests on assumptions imported by modern editors rather than on evidence from the songs' own tradition.", "why": "The text's pivot is Carbonneau's claim that the transcriptions 'owe less to medieval evidence than to their editors' expectations,' supported by the observations that no troubadour source prescribes modal rhythm and that the modes come from a distant repertory."}], "answer": 3},
{"id": "rw-cid-a4", "domain": "information", "skill": "central-ideas", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "The following text is adapted from an 1896 short story.\n\nMrs. Ashworth's devotion to music was acknowledged on all sides, and indeed she never spoke of the art without a reverence that was itself a kind of performance. She had endowed a subscription concert series whose programs she selected by the celebrity of the performers, and she attended each concert faithfully until the interval, when her carriage was invariably required elsewhere. Of the symphonies given in the second half of an evening she was nevertheless the town's most decisive judge, and no reputation in the city's musical life could be said to be settled until she had pronounced upon works she had been prevented, always regretfully, from hearing."}, "stem": "Based on the text, which choice best describes Mrs. Ashworth?", "choices": [{"text": "She is so burdened by social obligations that she is unable to enjoy the concerts she generously funds.", "why": "This takes the narrator's ironic excuses at face value; the mock-solemn 'invariably required elsewhere' and 'always regretfully' signal that her departures are chosen, not obligations that thwart genuine enjoyment.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "She has acquired an authority in the town's musical life that rests on displays of devotion rather than on genuine engagement with the music itself.", "why": "Every detail undercuts the surface praise—reverence that is 'itself a kind of performance,' programs chosen by celebrity, judgments 'pronounced' on works she never stayed to hear—so her standing is shown to rest on show rather than substance."}, {"text": "She conceals her extensive musical knowledge in order to appear modest before the town's performers.", "why": "The irony runs the other way: the text implies she lacks real musical engagement while posing as the town's 'most decisive judge,' not that she modestly hides expertise.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "She selects the performers for her concert series on the basis of their fame rather than their artistry.", "why": "The celebrity-based programming is stated, but it is one supporting detail of the portrait; it does not capture the text's larger characterization of her hollow authority.", "tag": "too-specific"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-cid-a5", "domain": "information", "skill": "central-ideas", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "For decades, the 'sailing stones' of Racetrack Playa, a dry lakebed in Death Valley, presented geologists with trails but never a culprit: rocks weighing as much as 300 kilograms plainly migrated across the flat surface, yet no one had seen one move. Proposed explanations tended toward the extravagant—hurricane-force winds, thick rafts of ice capable of floating the rocks. When researchers finally instrumented the playa with GPS-tagged stones and a weather station, the mechanism they recorded was almost disappointingly gentle: a shallow winter pond froze overnight into 'windowpane' ice a few millimeters thick, and as morning sun broke the sheet into large panels, light breezes drove the panels against the stones, nudging them along at a few meters per minute. That sequence of ordinary conditions arises so rarely in the desert that the motion had simply gone unwitnessed."}, "stem": "Which choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": [{"text": "The stones' movement, long attributed to extreme forces, turns out to require only a combination of unremarkable conditions—one that occurs so infrequently that the movement had escaped observation.", "why": "The text contrasts the 'extravagant' hypotheses with the 'almost disappointingly gentle' observed mechanism—millimeters-thick ice and light breezes—and closes by noting that the rarity of this ordinary sequence explains why motion went unwitnessed."}, {"text": "Geologists were unable to explain the stones' movement until instruments revealed that hurricane-force winds occasionally sweep across the playa.", "why": "Hurricane-force winds are named as one of the extravagant explanations the observations displaced; the recorded mechanism involved light breezes acting on thin ice panels.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "GPS tracking has become an essential tool for geologists studying processes that unfold too slowly or too rarely to be observed directly.", "why": "GPS tagging is the method of the single study described; the text makes no general claim about the role of GPS in geology.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "Winter ponds on Racetrack Playa can freeze overnight into sheets of ice that are only a few millimeters thick.", "why": "Accurate, but the windowpane ice is one link in the causal chain; the text's point is what the whole gentle mechanism reveals about the long-standing mystery.", "tag": "too-specific"}], "answer": 0},
{"id": "rw-cid-a6", "domain": "information", "skill": "central-ideas", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Between roughly 1905 and 1930, reproducing pianos such as the Welte-Mignon recorded the playing of eminent pianists onto perforated paper rolls, and manufacturers advertised the results as exact reincarnations of the artists' touch. Because the rolls preserve performances by musicians who died before electrical recording matured, scholars of performance practice have treated them as prized documents of a vanished style. Musicologist Daniel Okafor urges more caution: production files and surviving correspondence show that technicians routinely corrected wrong notes, regularized pedaling, and inserted dynamics after the recording session, sometimes with the pianist's approval and sometimes without. A roll, Okafor argues, is best understood not as a transparent trace of a performance but as a jointly authored artifact of performer, editor, and machine."}, "stem": "Which choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": [{"text": "Okafor has shown that piano rolls are worthless as evidence of historical performance because technicians altered them beyond recognition.", "why": "Okafor 'urges more caution' and reframes rolls as 'jointly authored' artifacts; neither he nor the text declares them worthless, and the rolls are still presented as preserving otherwise unrecorded playing.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Reproducing pianos preserved the playing of pianists who died before electrical recording technology matured.", "why": "True, and it explains why scholars prize the rolls, but it omits the text's central turn: Okafor's argument about editorial mediation of what the rolls record.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "Scholars of performance practice disagree about whether early twentieth-century pianists approved of the editorial changes made to their rolls.", "why": "The text reports that pianists sometimes approved edits and sometimes did not; it never describes a scholarly disagreement over that question.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Piano rolls give scholars rare access to early twentieth-century performance styles, but Okafor argues that editorial interventions mean they cannot be read as direct records of how pianists actually played.", "why": "The text establishes the rolls' value ('prized documents of a vanished style') and then pivots to Okafor's documented post-session interventions, concluding that a roll is 'not a transparent trace of a performance.'"}], "answer": 3},
{"id": "rw-cet-a1", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-textual", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 90, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Materials scientist Priya Raghunathan and colleagues observed that a severed sample of a polyurethane elastomer regains its original tensile strength far more quickly when its cut faces are rejoined in humid air than in dry air. The team hypothesizes that ambient water molecules act as transient molecular bridges: by forming hydrogen bonds with the polymer's severed chain ends, water temporarily tethers and aligns those ends, holding them in place until direct chain-to-chain hydrogen bonds can form and displace the water."}, "stem": "Which finding, if true, would most strongly support the team's hypothesis?", "choices": [{"text": "Samples of the elastomer healed in humid air ultimately regain a slightly greater share of their original tensile strength than samples healed in dry air do.", "why": "This merely re-documents the humidity advantage the hypothesis was devised to explain; it offers no evidence that water bonds to and bridges the severed chain ends.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "The elastomer's cut surfaces absorb a thin film of moisture within seconds of exposure to humid air.", "why": "Water being present at the cut faces is a precondition for any water-based explanation, but it is equally consistent with water simply softening the polymer; it does not show that water tethers chain ends via hydrogen bonds.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "A chemically similar elastomer whose chain ends have been modified so that they cannot form hydrogen bonds with water heals at the same slow rate in humid air as in dry air.", "why": "Correct. If the humidity advantage vanishes exactly when water can no longer hydrogen-bond to the chain ends, then the accelerated healing must depend on water bonding to those ends—precisely the transient-bridge mechanism the team proposes."}, {"text": "Several other self-healing polymers, including some that mend by re-forming covalent bonds rather than hydrogen bonds, also recover strength more rapidly in humid environments.", "why": "If polymers that heal without hydrogen bonding also speed up in humidity, water's effect is likely something generic, such as increased chain mobility—a result that weakens rather than supports the specific hydrogen-bond bridging mechanism.", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-cet-a2", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-textual", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 90, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "“‘Hope’ is the thing with feathers” is a poem by Emily Dickinson that was first published in 1891. In an essay on the poem, a literary scholar claims that Dickinson figures hope as a bird whose song is not merely able to endure adversity but is actually heightened by it, becoming most potent exactly when conditions are most punishing."}, "stem": "Which quotation from “‘Hope’ is the thing with feathers” most effectively illustrates the claim?", "choices": [{"text": "“And sweetest—in the Gale—is heard— / And sore must be the storm— / That could abash the little Bird / That kept so many warm—”", "why": "Correct. The song is “sweetest—in the Gale,” and only an extraordinarily “sore” storm could “abash” the bird: adversity makes hope's song stronger, not merely something it survives—exactly the intensification the scholar describes."}, {"text": "“‘Hope’ is the thing with feathers— / That perches in the soul— / And sings the tune without the words— / And never stops—at all—”", "why": "These lines establish that hope's song is ceaseless, but they mention no adversity whatsoever, so they cannot illustrate hardship heightening the song.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "“I've heard it in the chillest land— / And on the strangest Sea—”", "why": "These lines show hope's song persisting amid harsh settings—the mere endurance the scholar's claim explicitly moves beyond—but they say nothing about the song becoming more potent in those settings.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "“Yet—never—in Extremity, / It asked a crumb—of me.”", "why": "These lines concern what hope demands of the speaker (nothing), illustrating hope's selflessness rather than any effect of adversity on the power of its song.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}], "answer": 0},
{"id": "rw-cet-a3", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-textual", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 90, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "“The Weir,” a poem published anonymously in an 1874 issue of a London literary magazine, was long attributed to one of the many imitators of the Victorian poet Edith Marlowe, since it reproduces her characteristic diction and imagery. Literary historian Ines Vukovic argues that the poem is Marlowe's own, pointing to its handling of enjambment—the continuation of a sentence beyond the end of a poetic line—which closely matches Marlowe's practice. Vukovic's reasoning assumes that habits of enjambment, unlike choices of diction and imagery, operate largely below a poet's conscious awareness and thus resist deliberate imitation."}, "stem": "Which finding, if true, would most strongly support Vukovic's hypothesis?", "choices": [{"text": "“The Weir” contains several rare compound adjectives that appear nowhere else in Victorian poetry except in Marlowe's published collections.", "why": "Shared rare diction cannot discriminate between Marlowe and her imitators, who by the passage's own account reproduced her diction; it therefore does nothing to favor Vukovic's attribution over the rival explanation.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Marlowe's private notebooks reveal that she occasionally submitted poems to London magazines without signing them.", "why": "This makes anonymous publication by Marlowe plausible in general but supplies no evidence tying her to this poem and does not validate enjambment as a marker that imitators cannot fake.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Statistical analyses show that the frequency of enjambment in Marlowe's poems shifted markedly between her early and late collections.", "why": "If Marlowe's own enjambment varied widely over her career, the trait is not a stable signature, which weakens rather than strengthens an attribution based on an enjambment match.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "In experiments, poets asked to compose convincing imitations of other writers' verse consistently mimicked those writers' diction and imagery but unconsciously retained their own habits of enjambment.", "why": "Correct. This directly validates the assumption Vukovic's inference hinges on: imitators can copy diction and imagery but not enjambment, so the enjambment match points to Marlowe herself rather than to one of her imitators."}], "answer": 3},
{"id": "rw-cet-a4", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-textual", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 90, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Ancient Roman marine concrete is studded with millimeter-scale lime clasts, bright white lumps long dismissed as evidence of careless mixing. A team led by engineer Dario Ferrante instead proposes that the clasts constitute a self-healing system that accounts for the material's exceptional longevity in seawater: because the clasts are more brittle than the surrounding mortar, new cracks should propagate preferentially through them; seawater seeping into such a crack then dissolves calcium from the freshly exposed clast interior, and that calcium re-precipitates as calcite, sealing the crack."}, "stem": "Which finding, if true, would most directly undermine the team's hypothesis?", "choices": [{"text": "Modern concrete formulated without lime clasts develops initial cracks at roughly the same rate as replica Roman concrete that contains them.", "why": "This concerns how readily cracks form, but the hypothesis concerns what happens after cracks form; equal rates of crack initiation leave the proposed healing mechanism untouched.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Microscopic imaging of cracked samples of ancient Roman marine concrete shows that cracks overwhelmingly deflect around lime clasts rather than passing through them.", "why": "Correct. The proposed mechanism requires cracks to travel through the brittle clasts so that reactive calcium is exposed to seawater; if cracks detour around the clasts, the causal chain never begins, and the self-healing account collapses."}, {"text": "Chemical analysis reveals that the calcite filling cracks in ancient Roman piers is compositionally consistent with calcium derived from lime clasts.", "why": "Calcite traceable to the clasts is exactly what the hypothesis predicts, so this finding supports rather than undermines the team's account.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "Some inland Roman structures built with mortar lacking lime clasts have also remained largely intact for two millennia.", "why": "This shows only that other routes to durability exist; the hypothesis explains the longevity of clast-bearing marine concrete and does not claim that clasts are the sole possible source of endurance.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-cet-b1", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-textual", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 90, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "The staghorn coral Acropora cervicornis hosts single-celled algae that supply it with nutrients but that it expels—an event called bleaching—when seawater grows too warm. Marine biologist Nadia Okonkwo noticed that during a 2023 heat wave, colonies harboring an algal strain she labeled SC-7 bleached far less than colonies hosting other strains. She proposed that SC-7 protects its host because the strain produces unusually high levels of an antioxidant that neutralizes the reactive oxygen molecules which accumulate in heat-stressed algae and trigger the coral to expel them."}, "stem": "Which finding, if true, would most strongly support Okonkwo's hypothesis?", "choices": [{"text": "Genetic sequencing confirms that SC-7 is a distinct strain not found in any of the other coral colonies that were studied.", "why": "Being genetically distinct says nothing about heat protection; strains can differ genetically without conferring any advantage, so this leaves the proposed antioxidant mechanism untouched.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Under normal seawater temperatures, corals hosting SC-7 receive more nutrients from their algae than corals hosting other strains do.", "why": "A nutritional advantage under normal conditions does not address the heat-stress mechanism—neutralizing reactive oxygen molecules—that the hypothesis rests on.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "During laboratory heat stress, corals hosting SC-7 accumulated far fewer reactive oxygen molecules than corals hosting other strains, and chemically disabling SC-7's antioxidant caused those corals to bleach just as readily as the rest.", "why": "Directly confirms the proposed mechanism: SC-7 colonies build up fewer reactive oxygen molecules, and disabling the antioxidant removes the protection, establishing that the antioxidant is what prevents bleaching."}, {"text": "When seawater temperatures rose, corals hosting SC-7 expelled their algae sooner than corals hosting other strains did.", "why": "Expelling algae sooner is bleaching more, not less, so this contradicts both Okonkwo's observation and her hypothesis.", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-cet-b2", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-textual", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 90, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "To combat rising summer temperatures, the transportation department of a large city coated dozens of residential streets with a light-colored, reflective \"cool pavement\" sealant. Urban researcher Priya Mehta predicted that the treated streets would lower daytime air temperatures in the surrounding neighborhoods. Her reasoning was that the reflective coating would send incoming sunlight back upward into the atmosphere rather than allowing the dark asphalt beneath to absorb that energy and radiate it into the air as heat."}, "stem": "Which finding, if true, would most strongly undermine Mehta's prediction?", "choices": [{"text": "Sensors showed that most of the sunlight reflected from the coated streets was absorbed by adjacent building walls and pedestrians before escaping upward, and daytime air temperatures in the treated neighborhoods were no lower than they had been before the coating was applied.", "why": "Attacks both the mechanism and the outcome: if the reflected sunlight is reabsorbed nearby instead of escaping to the atmosphere, the coating cannot cool the air, and the measured air temperatures did not fall."}, {"text": "The surface temperature of the coated pavement itself measured several degrees lower than that of untreated asphalt on the same afternoons.", "why": "A cooler pavement surface is consistent with the reflection mechanism and would tend to support, not undermine, the prediction.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "The reflective sealant faded within two summers and had to be reapplied at considerable expense.", "why": "Cost and durability say nothing about whether the coating lowers air temperature while it is intact, which is what the prediction concerns.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "In a different city, coating rooftops with reflective paint reduced indoor temperatures in the buildings below.", "why": "Reflective roofs cooling building interiors is a separate intervention and outcome; it does not bear on street-level air temperature produced by cool pavement.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 0},
{"id": "rw-cet-b3", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-textual", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 90, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "The mimic octopus (Thaumoctopus mimicus) can alter its shape, color, and movement to impersonate other sea creatures. In an article about the species, marine biologist Lars Halvorsen argues that the octopus does not simply run through a fixed repertoire of impressions; rather, it selects a particular impersonation suited to the specific threat confronting it at the moment."}, "stem": "Which quotation from the article would most effectively illustrate Halvorsen's claim?", "choices": [{"text": "\"This remarkable animal can reproduce the appearance of at least fifteen different marine species, a range unmatched by any other known cephalopod.\"", "why": "A large repertoire demonstrates range, not that the octopus matches each impression to the specific threat, which is the heart of Halvorsen's claim.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "\"Its impersonations are so precise that they routinely deceive even the divers and biologists who have spent years studying the very animals being imitated.\"", "why": "The accuracy of the impersonations is a different property; it does not show that the choice of impersonation is tailored to the particular threat.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "\"The octopus tends to cycle through its impressions in a set order, presenting the same series of animals no matter what is happening around it.\"", "why": "A fixed order regardless of surroundings is precisely the 'fixed repertoire' behavior Halvorsen denies, so it contradicts the claim.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "\"When aggressive damselfish approached, the octopus mimicked a banded sea snake, a predator those fish flee; but when a snake-hunting predator arrived instead, it abruptly switched to imitating a venomous lionfish.\"", "why": "Shows the octopus selecting different impersonations matched to different specific threats—sea snake for the damselfish, lionfish for the snake-hunter—precisely illustrating threat-tailored choice."}], "answer": 3},
{"id": "rw-cet-b4", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-textual", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 90, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Facing a shortage of rental housing, a city imposed an annual tax on any residential unit left unoccupied for more than six months. Economist Daniel Ferreira predicted that the measure would reduce average rents across the city. His reasoning was that the tax would give owners of long-empty units a financial incentive to put them on the rental market, thereby enlarging the supply of available apartments that renters could choose among."}, "stem": "Which finding, if true, would most strongly support Ferreira's prediction?", "choices": [{"text": "In the year after the tax took effect, average rents across the city declined slightly.", "why": "A bare citywide decline is a correlation that could arise from many causes; it does not show that the tax worked through the vacant-unit supply mechanism Ferreira described.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "After the tax, thousands of previously long-vacant units were listed for rent, and the neighborhoods that gained the most such listings saw the steepest declines in average rent compared with neighborhoods that gained few.", "why": "Confirms the mechanism with a dose-response: formerly vacant units actually reached the market, and rents fell most where those new listings concentrated, linking the supply increase to lower rents."}, {"text": "The tax raised substantial revenue, which the city allocated to constructing new public housing.", "why": "Funding new construction is a separate mechanism from the incentive to rent out existing vacant units and does not support Ferreira's specific reasoning.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Many owners of vacant units chose to pay the tax and leave their units empty, and few additional apartments reached the rental market.", "why": "If the supply never grows, the mechanism fails; this outcome undermines rather than supports the prediction.", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-cet-c1", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-textual", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 90, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Commercial tomato varieties are typically far less sweet than the heirloom varieties prized by home gardeners. Food scientist Marisol Argento attributes this difference to an unintended consequence of twentieth-century breeding: to make fruits ripen to a uniform red, growers selected plants carrying a mutation that eliminates the dark-green \"shoulder\" on unripe fruit. Argento contends that this same mutation disables a gene that would otherwise direct the developing fruit to manufacture sugars—so the very trait that gave commercial tomatoes their even color also stripped them of their sweetness."}, "stem": "Which finding, if true, would most strongly support Argento's hypothesis?", "choices": [{"text": "In blind taste tests, most consumers preferred the flavor of heirloom tomatoes to that of leading commercial varieties.", "why": "This restates the background preference for heirlooms without touching Argento's proposed cause—the mutation and the disabled sugar gene—so it cannot confirm her mechanism.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Heirloom tomatoes bruise more easily and spoil faster than commercial varieties bred to ripen uniformly.", "why": "A true contrast in durability, but shelf life has no bearing on whether the uniform-ripening mutation is what reduces sugar.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "When researchers inserted a functioning copy of the disabled gene into a uniformly ripening commercial variety, the fruit developed uneven coloring but accumulated markedly more sugar than unmodified plants did.", "why": "This tests the exact causal link Argento names: restoring the gene simultaneously brings back the green shoulder (uneven color) and the lost sugar, showing that the uniform-ripening mutation is what suppresses sweetness."}, {"text": "A commercial variety carrying the uniform-ripening mutation produced just as much sugar as heirloom varieties when both were grown in nutrient-rich soil.", "why": "If the mutation can coexist with normal sugar levels, the finding cuts against Argento's mechanism rather than supporting it.", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-cet-c2", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-textual", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 90, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Katherine Dunham trained as an anthropologist before founding one of the first self-supporting Black dance companies in the United States. In an article about her choreography, a dance historian argues that what most distinguished Dunham's early stage works was not merely their debt to the Caribbean social dances she had documented in the field, but her deliberate reworking of those vernacular forms into the disciplined vocabulary of the concert stage."}, "stem": "Which quotation from the article would most effectively illustrate the dance historian's claim?", "choices": [{"text": "\"In Rites de Passage, Dunham took the hip isolations and grounded weight of the Haitian ritual dances she had recorded in her field notebooks and set them within the measured phrasing and formal architecture of a proscenium work.\"", "why": "It shows both halves of the claim at once: vernacular movement documented in her anthropological fieldwork (Haitian dances from her notebooks) deliberately reworked into concert-stage form (measured phrasing, the formal architecture of a proscenium work)."}, {"text": "\"Dunham's months of fieldwork in Haiti and Jamaica produced meticulous records of social dances that scholars had previously dismissed as unworthy of study.\"", "why": "This captures only her documentation of vernacular dance and says nothing about reworking it for the concert stage, so it illustrates just half of the two-part claim.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "\"By the 1940s Dunham's troupe was touring internationally and supporting itself through ticket sales, a rarity for Black artists of the era.\"", "why": "A fact about the company's commercial success, unrelated to how she transformed vernacular dances into concert choreography.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "\"Dunham took care to present the Caribbean dances exactly as she had witnessed them, preserving every gesture unaltered so that audiences saw the traditions in their original form.\"", "why": "This contradicts the claim's core point—that she deliberately reworked the vernacular forms—by insisting she left them unaltered.", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 0},
{"id": "rw-cet-c3", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-textual", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 90, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Striga, a parasitic weed, devastates maize harvests across sub-Saharan Africa by attaching to the crop's roots. Agronomist Nadia Okonkwo has studied a \"push-pull\" method in which farmers plant the legume Desmodium between rows of maize, a practice that sharply reduces Striga infestation. Okonkwo proposes a specific explanation: Desmodium's roots release chemical compounds into the soil that cause dormant Striga seeds to germinate prematurely, before any maize roots are available for the young weeds to latch onto. Deprived of a host, these seedlings soon die."}, "stem": "Which finding, if true, would most strongly undermine Okonkwo's explanation?", "choices": [{"text": "Soil collected from beneath Desmodium plants contained unusually high concentrations of the compounds known to trigger Striga seeds to germinate.", "why": "This strengthens Okonkwo's chemical mechanism rather than undermining it—the opposite of what the question asks.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "Maize intercropped with Desmodium produced higher yields even in Striga-free fields, because the legume enriched the soil with nitrogen.", "why": "A genuine second benefit of the legume, but soil fertility has no bearing on whether the root chemicals are what suppress Striga.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Farmers who adopted the push-pull method reported spending less on chemical herbicides than farmers who did not.", "why": "An economic outcome that says nothing about the biological mechanism Okonkwo proposes.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "In plots where a barrier kept Desmodium's root compounds from spreading into the surrounding soil, Striga was suppressed as effectively as in plots without the barrier.", "why": "If suppression persists even when the root chemicals cannot reach the Striga seeds, premature \"suicidal\" germination cannot be what drives the effect—directly undercutting Okonkwo's proposed mechanism."}], "answer": 3},
{"id": "rw-cet-c4", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-textual", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 90, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "The German playwright and director Bertolt Brecht is often remembered for the Marxist convictions that animate his plays. But in an article on his methods, a theater historian contends that Brecht's most consequential contribution to modern staging lay elsewhere: in an array of techniques designed to keep spectators from losing themselves in the story and to remind them, at every moment, that they were watching a deliberate theatrical construction."}, "stem": "Which quotation from the article would most effectively illustrate the theater historian's claim?", "choices": [{"text": "\"Brecht held that the theater should do more than entertain—it should awaken in its audiences a critical awareness of social and economic injustice.\"", "why": "This describes the political aims the historian explicitly sets aside (\"lay elsewhere\") and names no staging technique at all, so it does not illustrate the claim.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "\"In The Caucasian Chalk Circle, a Singer stood at the edge of the stage narrating the action aloud and announcing each character's next move before the actors performed it, so that no spectator could mistake the events for anything but a story being told.\"", "why": "This is a concrete staging device that continually reminds the audience it is watching a deliberate construction—precisely the kind of technique the historian identifies as Brecht's key contribution."}, {"text": "\"Brecht's finest productions could move audiences to tears, drawing them so completely into the characters' fates that the house fell silent.\"", "why": "It describes total absorption in the story—the very immersion the claim says Brecht's techniques were designed to prevent.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "\"Fleeing the Nazi regime, Brecht wrote several of his major plays in exile before returning to found the Berliner Ensemble in East Berlin.\"", "why": "A biographical fact about his career that illustrates none of the illusion-breaking techniques the claim concerns.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-ceq-a1", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-quant", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "chart", "text": "Cognitive psychologist Dana Merced and colleagues had college students learn 40 Swahili–English word pairs and then either restudy the pairs or practice retrieving them from memory; recall was then tested after 5 minutes, 2 days, or 1 week. Merced's team hypothesized that retrieval practice strengthens long-term retention specifically, conferring little or no advantage when memory is tested almost immediately. The results were consistent with this hypothesis: ______", "chart": {"type": "groupedBar", "title": "Mean Recall of Swahili–English Word Pairs by Study Strategy and Test Delay", "xLabel": "Test delay", "yLabel": "Word pairs recalled (%)", "categories": ["5 minutes", "2 days", "1 week"], "series": [{"name": "Restudy", "values": [81, 54, 42]}, {"name": "Retrieval practice", "values": [76, 68, 61]}], "yFormat": "percent"}}, "stem": "Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the text?", "choices": [{"text": "participants who practiced retrieval recalled a larger percentage of word pairs than restudy participants at the 5-minute test but a smaller percentage at the 1-week test.", "why": "This reverses the pattern in the graph: retrieval practice trailed restudy at 5 minutes (76% versus 81%) and led at 1 week (61% versus 42%).", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "participants who practiced retrieval recalled 68% of the word pairs after 2 days and 61% after 1 week, whereas restudy participants recalled 54% and 42%, respectively.", "why": "These figures are accurate, but they report only the delayed tests; without the 5-minute comparison, they cannot show that retrieval practice confers little or no advantage on an almost immediate test, which the hypothesis requires.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "participants who practiced retrieval recalled a smaller percentage of word pairs than restudy participants at the 5-minute test (76% versus 81%) but a larger percentage at both the 2-day and 1-week tests.", "why": "This is the only choice that captures both parts of the hypothesis: no immediate advantage (76% versus 81% at 5 minutes) plus a long-term advantage (68% versus 54% at 2 days; 61% versus 42% at 1 week), exactly matching the graph."}, {"text": "in both the restudy group and the retrieval practice group, participants recalled a smaller percentage of word pairs as the test delay increased.", "why": "Accurately describes the graph (both series decline as delay increases), but a general decline over time says nothing about the relative advantage of retrieval practice, so it cannot support the hypothesis.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-ceq-a2", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-quant", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "chart", "text": "Constructing a building generates \"embodied carbon\"—emissions from producing and transporting materials such as concrete and steel. Architectural engineer Pieter Vos compared the average embodied carbon of newly constructed buildings in the Netherlands with that of comparable adaptive-reuse projects, in which existing structures are converted to new uses, across three building types. Vos concluded that although adaptive reuse lowered embodied carbon for every building type studied, the size of the reduction differed sharply by type, with office conversions offering by far the greatest savings.", "chart": {"type": "groupedBar", "title": "Average Embodied Carbon by Construction Approach and Building Type", "xLabel": "Building type", "yLabel": "Embodied carbon (kg CO2e per square meter)", "categories": ["Office", "School", "Warehouse"], "series": [{"name": "New construction", "values": [485, 410, 350]}, {"name": "Adaptive reuse", "values": [210, 265, 300]}], "yFormat": "number"}}, "stem": "Which choice best describes data from the graph that support Vos's conclusion?", "choices": [{"text": "Adaptive reuse was associated with lower embodied carbon than new construction for all three building types, but the difference was far larger for offices (485 versus 210 kg CO2e per square meter) than for schools or warehouses.", "why": "This is the only choice that establishes both parts of Vos's conclusion: reuse is lower in every category, and the gap is largest for offices (275 kg CO2e per square meter, versus 145 for schools and 50 for warehouses)."}, {"text": "Adaptive reuse reduced average embodied carbon by more than half for each of the three building types.", "why": "The graph contradicts this: only for offices is the reuse value less than half the new-construction value (210 versus 485); for schools (265 versus 410) and warehouses (300 versus 350) the reduction is well under half.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Adaptive-reuse office projects averaged 210 kg CO2e per square meter of embodied carbon, whereas newly constructed office buildings averaged 485 kg CO2e per square meter.", "why": "These office figures are accurate, but data from a single building type cannot show that reuse lowered embodied carbon for every type or that the savings differed sharply across types.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "The difference in embodied carbon between the two construction approaches was smallest for offices and largest for warehouses.", "why": "This reverses the graph: the gap is largest for offices (275 kg CO2e per square meter) and smallest for warehouses (50).", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 0},
{"id": "rw-ceq-a3", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-quant", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "chart", "text": "Neuroscientist Yuko Tanabe and colleagues trained two groups of participants on a finger-tapping sequence task. One group trained in the morning and was retested 12 hours later, after a day spent awake; the other trained in the evening and was retested 12 hours later, after a night of sleep. Because the retention interval was identical for the two groups, Tanabe's team argued that any additional improvement in the sleep group would reflect sleep-dependent memory consolidation rather than the mere passage of time—an interpretation the results support, in that ______", "chart": {"type": "groupedBar", "title": "Performance on a Finger-Tapping Sequence Task", "xLabel": "Test point", "yLabel": "Correct sequences per 30-second trial", "categories": ["End of training", "Retest 12 hours later"], "series": [{"name": "Wake group", "values": [24.1, 24.6]}, {"name": "Sleep group", "values": [23.8, 28.5]}], "yFormat": "number"}}, "stem": "Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the text?", "choices": [{"text": "the sleep group produced 28.5 correct sequences per trial at the retest, an improvement over the 23.8 correct sequences it produced at the end of training.", "why": "Accurate for the sleep group, but without the wake group's data an improvement over 12 hours could still reflect the mere passage of time—the very alternative Tanabe's design was meant to rule out.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "the wake group showed a substantial gain at the retest, while the sleep group's performance was essentially unchanged from the end of training.", "why": "This reverses the two series: it was the sleep group that gained substantially (23.8 to 28.5) while the wake group was nearly flat (24.1 to 24.6).", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "the two groups performed at nearly the same level at the end of training, with 24.1 correct sequences for the wake group and 23.8 for the sleep group.", "why": "Accurately describes the baseline, but similar end-of-training performance alone says nothing about what happened over the retention interval, so it cannot show a sleep-dependent gain.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "the sleep group improved from 23.8 to 28.5 correct sequences per trial between the end of training and the retest, whereas the wake group's performance remained nearly unchanged over the same interval.", "why": "This is the only choice that uses both series across an identical 12-hour interval: the sleep group's gain (23.8 to 28.5) alongside the wake group's near-flat performance (24.1 to 24.6) isolates sleep, not elapsed time, as the source of improvement."}], "answer": 3},
{"id": "rw-ceq-a4", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-quant", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "chart", "text": "\"Active design\" guidelines encourage architects to promote everyday physical activity—for example, by making stairs more inviting than elevators. When a university renovated one of two nearly identical office buildings at the end of May, replacing an enclosed fire stair with a daylit staircase visible from the lobby, public-health researcher Amara Okafor tracked the percentage of upward trips made by stairs in both buildings throughout the year. Okafor concluded that the design change itself—not a campus-wide factor such as a concurrent employee-wellness campaign—drove an increase in stair use.", "chart": {"type": "line", "title": "Percentage of Upward Trips Made by Stairs, by Month", "xLabel": "Month", "yLabel": "Trips made by stairs (%)", "categories": ["January", "March", "May", "July", "September", "November"], "series": [{"name": "Renovated building", "values": [12, 13, 14, 23, 25, 26]}, {"name": "Unrenovated building", "values": [11, 12, 12, 13, 12, 13]}], "yFormat": "percent", "note": "The renovation was completed at the end of May."}}, "stem": "Which choice best describes data from the graph that support Okafor's conclusion?", "choices": [{"text": "In the renovated building, the percentage of upward trips made by stairs nearly doubled between May (14%) and November (26%).", "why": "Accurate for the renovated building, but on its own this increase is equally consistent with a campus-wide cause such as the wellness campaign; ruling that out requires the unrenovated building's data.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "In the renovated building, the percentage of upward trips made by stairs rose from 14% in May to 23% in July and remained elevated for the rest of the year, while in the unrenovated building it stayed between 11% and 13% all year.", "why": "This is the only choice that pairs the jump after the May renovation (14% to 23%, then 25% and 26%) with the unrenovated building's flat 11–13% range, showing the increase followed the design change rather than any campus-wide factor."}, {"text": "Following the completion of the renovation in May, the percentage of upward trips made by stairs increased sharply in both buildings.", "why": "The graph contradicts this: only the renovated building's percentage rose sharply after May, while the unrenovated building's stayed within its earlier 11–13% range.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "In the unrenovated building, the percentage of upward trips made by stairs was slightly higher in November (13%) than in January (11%).", "why": "An accurate reading, but it concerns only the unrenovated building's small fluctuation and includes no data about the renovated building, so it cannot show that the design change increased stair use.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-ceq-b1", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-quant", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "chart", "text": "A comet's outgassing—the release of gases as its ices warm—generally intensifies as the comet nears the Sun. From 2014 to 2016, instruments aboard the European Space Agency's Rosetta spacecraft monitored the rates at which comet 67P/Churyumov–Gerasimenko released water vapor and carbon dioxide before and after perihelion, the comet's closest approach to the Sun. Planetary scientist Ilse Brandt and colleagues concluded that the comet's water emission was controlled mainly by the momentary intensity of solar heating, whereas its carbon dioxide emission—fed by ices buried deeper beneath the surface—responded to that heating only after a substantial delay. Rosetta's measurements are consistent with this account, showing that ______", "chart": {"type": "line", "title": "Outgassing Rates of Comet 67P Before and After Perihelion", "xLabel": "Months relative to perihelion", "yLabel": "Emission rate (10^27 molecules per second)", "categories": ["-4", "-2", "0 (perihelion)", "+2", "+4", "+6"], "series": [{"name": "Water vapor", "values": [3, 9, 15, 9, 4, 2]}, {"name": "Carbon dioxide", "values": [2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 7]}], "yFormat": "number", "note": "Rates estimated from measurements by Rosetta's onboard spectrometers."}}, "stem": "Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the text?", "choices": [{"text": "carbon dioxide emission roughly doubled from two months before perihelion to two months after perihelion.", "why": "Accurately reflects the carbon dioxide values (4 versus 8), but it describes only carbon dioxide; without any comparison to water, it cannot show that the two gases responded to solar heating on different schedules, which is what Brandt's conclusion requires.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "carbon dioxide emission reached its maximum at perihelion, whereas water emission continued to rise for four months afterward.", "why": "Reverses the two series: the graph shows water peaking at perihelion (15) and carbon dioxide not peaking until four months after perihelion (10).", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "water emission peaked at perihelion and then declined as the comet receded from the Sun, whereas carbon dioxide emission continued to climb after perihelion, not reaching its maximum until four months later.", "why": "Correct. The water series peaks at perihelion (15) and falls to 4 and then 2 afterward, while the carbon dioxide series keeps rising past perihelion to its maximum (10) at +4 months—exactly the immediate response versus delayed response that Brandt's conclusion describes."}, {"text": "water emission exceeded carbon dioxide emission at every point during the observation period.", "why": "Contradicted by the graph: at four and six months after perihelion, carbon dioxide emission (10 and 7) exceeds water emission (4 and 2). Water leads only in the earlier portion of the encounter.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-ceq-b2", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-quant", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "chart", "text": "Surveys of exoplanets that orbit close to their stars have catalogued worlds ranging from smaller than Earth to larger than Neptune. Astronomer Priya Raghunathan and colleagues argue that intense stellar radiation sculpts this population through photoevaporation: a planet born with a thick hydrogen-rich atmosphere either retains it, remaining comparatively large, or has it stripped away entirely, leaving a small bare rocky core. Because a partially stripped atmosphere is unstable, the process should leave few planets at intermediate sizes, carving a \"valley\" into the size distribution. The team contends that census data on short-period exoplanets bear out this prediction, since ______", "chart": {"type": "bar", "title": "Confirmed Short-Period Exoplanets by Size", "xLabel": "Planet radius (Earth radii)", "yLabel": "Number of planets", "categories": ["1.0–1.5", "1.5–2.0", "2.0–2.5", "2.5–3.0", "3.0–3.5"], "series": [{"name": "Confirmed planets", "values": [340, 130, 310, 220, 90]}], "yFormat": "number"}}, "stem": "Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the text?", "choices": [{"text": "there are roughly 130 known short-period planets with radii between 1.5 and 2.0 times Earth's, far fewer than the roughly 340 and 310 planets in the size ranges immediately below and above that interval.", "why": "Correct. The prediction is a deficit at intermediate sizes flanked by more populous ranges on both sides, and only this choice cites that specific dip: 130 planets in the 1.5–2.0 range versus 340 and 310 in the adjacent ranges."}, {"text": "the number of known short-period planets declines steadily with increasing radius, from about 340 planets in the smallest size range to about 90 in the largest.", "why": "The endpoints are accurate, but the decline is not steady—the count drops to 130, rebounds to 310, then falls again—and a smooth decline would show no valley at all, so this reading would actually fail to support the prediction.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "roughly equal numbers of known short-period planets have radii between 1.0 and 1.5 times Earth's and radii between 2.0 and 2.5 times Earth's.", "why": "Accurately compares the two flanking ranges (340 versus 310), but it omits the decisive fact—the scarcity of planets in the 1.5–2.0 range between them—so it provides no evidence of a valley.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "planets with radii between 1.5 and 2.0 times Earth's outnumber planets in any other size range in the census.", "why": "Reverses the trend the graph shows: the 1.5–2.0 range (130 planets) is markedly less populous than the ranges on either side of it, not the most populous range in the census.", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 0},
{"id": "rw-ceq-b3", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-quant", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "chart", "text": "In late 2019, the red supergiant Betelgeuse began to dim so dramatically that some observers speculated the star was nearing a supernova. Astronomers advanced two more mundane explanations: an unusually cool patch on the star's surface, which would diminish the star's output across all wavelengths, or a cloud of dust condensing just in front of the star, which would block much of its visible light while allowing most of its infrared radiation to pass. Astrophysicist Miguel Andrade and colleagues, comparing brightness measurements at both wavelengths across the dimming episode, argue that the observations favor the dust explanation, noting that ______", "chart": {"type": "line", "title": "Brightness of Betelgeuse, October 2019–June 2020", "xLabel": "Month", "yLabel": "Brightness (percent of typical value)", "categories": ["Oct. 2019", "Dec. 2019", "Feb. 2020", "Apr. 2020", "June 2020"], "series": [{"name": "Visible light", "values": [95, 70, 40, 75, 95]}, {"name": "Infrared", "values": [100, 98, 97, 98, 100]}], "yFormat": "percent"}}, "stem": "Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the text?", "choices": [{"text": "Betelgeuse's visible brightness declined from about 95 percent of its typical value in October 2019 to about 40 percent in February 2020.", "why": "Accurately reads the visible-light series, but a steep visible decline is equally consistent with the cool-patch explanation; without the infrared comparison, it cannot favor one explanation over the other.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Betelgeuse's infrared brightness declined more steeply between October 2019 and February 2020 than its visible brightness did.", "why": "Reverses the series: over that span, visible brightness fell from 95 to 40 percent while infrared brightness fell only from 100 to 97 percent.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "Betelgeuse's visible brightness dropped below 25 percent of its typical value before beginning to recover in the spring of 2020.", "why": "Misstates the data: the visible-light series never falls below its February 2020 minimum of about 40 percent of the typical value.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Betelgeuse's visible brightness fell to about 40 percent of its typical value by February 2020, while its infrared brightness never fell below about 97 percent of its typical value.", "why": "Correct. The dust explanation predicts dimming in visible light but not in infrared, and only this choice captures both series: the visible minimum of 40 percent alongside infrared values that stay at 97 percent or above throughout the episode."}], "answer": 3},
{"id": "rw-ceq-b4", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-quant", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "chart", "text": "Bram Stoker's 1897 novel Dracula unfolds entirely through documents—journals, letters, phonograph transcriptions, newspaper clippings—produced by its characters. Because the novel opens with Jonathan Harker's journal of his journey to Transylvania, Dracula is often remembered as primarily Harker's story. Literary scholar Eleanor Vasquez, who tabulated the share of the novel's words attributable to each narrator, concludes that this perception reflects only the novel's opening: after dominating the early chapters, Harker's journal recedes to a minor role, and Dr. Seward's diary becomes the novel's principal narrative vehicle.", "chart": {"type": "groupedBar", "title": "Share of Words in Dracula Narrated by Three Characters, by Quarter of the Novel", "xLabel": "Portion of the novel", "yLabel": "Percent of the novel's words", "categories": ["First quarter", "Second quarter", "Third quarter", "Fourth quarter"], "series": [{"name": "Jonathan Harker's journal", "values": [45, 10, 8, 12]}, {"name": "Dr. Seward's diary", "values": [20, 40, 42, 38]}, {"name": "Mina Harker's journal", "values": [10, 25, 28, 22]}], "yFormat": "percent", "note": "Percentages within each quarter do not sum to 100 because the novel also contains letters, newspaper clippings, and other documents."}}, "stem": "Which choice best describes data from the graph that support Vasquez's conclusion?", "choices": [{"text": "Mina Harker's journal accounts for a larger share of the novel's words in the second quarter than in the first quarter.", "why": "Accurately reads Mina Harker's series (25 percent versus 10 percent), but Vasquez's conclusion concerns Jonathan Harker's decline and Seward's rise; data about Mina's journal alone address neither part of the claim.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Harker's journal accounts for about 45 percent of the words in the novel's first quarter but no more than about 12 percent in any later quarter, in each of which Seward's diary contributes the largest share of the three narrators.", "why": "Correct. Vasquez's two-part claim—early dominance followed by recession, with Seward's diary taking over—is matched exactly: Harker falls from 45 percent to 10, 8, and 12 percent, while Seward's diary leads all three narrators in each later quarter (40, 42, and 38 percent)."}, {"text": "Seward's diary contributes the largest share of the novel's words in the first quarter, while Harker's journal contributes the largest share in each of the three later quarters.", "why": "Reverses the graph: Harker's journal leads in the first quarter (45 percent versus Seward's 20 percent), and Seward's diary leads in each of the later quarters.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "Harker's journal accounts for a smaller share of the novel's words than either Seward's diary or Mina Harker's journal in every quarter of the novel.", "why": "Contradicted by the first-quarter data, where Harker's journal (45 percent) far exceeds both other narrators—and Harker's early dominance is essential to Vasquez's claim that the popular perception reflects the novel's opening.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-ceq-c1", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-quant", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "chart", "text": "Many birds cache seeds in autumn to survive the following winter, but caching is costly: stored food can be pilfered by rivals or ruined by moisture. Ecologist Mara Devlin measured the caching rates, in seeds stored per hour, of four seed-hoarding species in a montane forest, first during autumn and again during the following winter. Although every species cached more actively in autumn than in winter, Devlin found that the size of the drop was far from uniform: the species whose caching rate declined most sharply from autumn to winter was ______", "chart": {"type": "groupedBar", "title": "Seed-Caching Rates of Four Bird Species by Season", "xLabel": "Species", "yLabel": "Seeds cached per hour", "categories": ["Clark's nutcracker", "Pinyon jay", "Scrub jay", "Black-billed magpie"], "series": [{"name": "Autumn", "values": [44, 52, 26, 21]}, {"name": "Winter", "values": [9, 40, 6, 18]}], "yFormat": "number", "note": "Rates are mean values recorded across the montane study site."}}, "stem": "Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the sentence?", "choices": [{"text": "the pinyon jay.", "why": "Pinyon jays had the highest autumn rate (52 seeds per hour), but their rate fell by only 12 (52 to 40); this mistakes a peak value for the sharpest decline.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "the Clark's nutcracker.", "why": "The nutcracker's rate fell from 44 to 9 seeds per hour, a drop of 35—larger than any other species' decline—so it declined most sharply from autumn to winter."}, {"text": "the scrub jay.", "why": "Scrub jays had the lowest winter rate (6 seeds per hour), but their decline was only 20 (26 to 6); a low endpoint is not the same as the largest drop.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "the black-billed magpie.", "why": "Magpies showed the smallest decline of all (21 to 18, a drop of just 3), the opposite of the sharpest decline.", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-ceq-c2", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-quant", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "chart", "text": "Economists once assumed that participation in paid work falls off steadily as people approach and then pass the traditional retirement age. Drawing on national labor surveys collected every five years from 2000 to 2020, economist Idris Fanaka compared the labor force participation rates of two older cohorts—workers aged 55 to 64 and workers aged 65 and older. After examining the trends for both cohorts, Fanaka determined that ______", "chart": {"type": "line", "title": "Labor Force Participation Rate by Age Cohort, 2000–2020", "xLabel": "Year", "yLabel": "Labor force participation rate (%)", "categories": ["2000", "2005", "2010", "2015", "2020"], "series": [{"name": "Ages 55–64", "values": [60, 62, 64, 65, 66]}, {"name": "Ages 65 and older", "values": [12, 15, 19, 23, 27]}], "yFormat": "number"}}, "stem": "Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the sentence?", "choices": [{"text": "in every year shown, the participation rate of workers aged 55 to 64 was at least four times that of workers aged 65 and older.", "why": "This holds in 2000 (60 vs. 12) and 2005 (62 vs. 15) but fails by 2010 (64 vs. 19, under four times), so it overgeneralizes an early-years relationship.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "from 2000 to 2020, the participation rate of workers aged 55 to 64 rose by more percentage points than that of workers aged 65 and older did.", "why": "The 55–64 rate rose 6 points (60 to 66) while the 65-and-older rate rose 15 points (12 to 27), so the comparison is backward.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "from 2010 to 2020, the gap between the two groups' participation rates narrowed by more than half.", "why": "The gap went from 45 points (64 minus 19) to 39 points (66 minus 27)—a narrowing of just 6 points, nowhere near half.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "from 2000 to 2020, the participation rate of workers aged 65 and older more than doubled.", "why": "The rate for workers 65 and older climbed from 12 to 27; because 27 exceeds twice 12 (24), it more than doubled."}], "answer": 3},
{"id": "rw-inf-a1", "domain": "information", "skill": "inferences", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Traditional Polynesian navigators can cross vast stretches of open ocean without instruments, in part by detecting subtle patterns in ocean swells as the swells bend around islands. In one study, trainees who had learned to navigate within a single archipelago reliably identified the swell patterns surrounding those familiar islands. When tested near unfamiliar islands—whose surrounding seafloor produced differently shaped swells—the same trainees performed no better than chance. These results suggest that the swell-reading ability the trainees developed"}, "stem": "Which choice most logically completes the text?", "choices": [{"text": "enables experienced navigators to rely on ocean swells rather than on the stars when they approach unfamiliar islands.", "why": "The passage never mentions stars or contrasts swell reading with other techniques, and it studies trainees, not experienced navigators; this conclusion has no basis in the premises.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "cannot be acquired by anyone who has not personally sailed among the islands being navigated.", "why": "The trainees learned within an archipelago, but the text gives no basis for an absolute claim about who “cannot” ever acquire the skill; “any… who has not” overreaches the evidence.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "may reflect familiarity with the features of particular islands rather than a general skill in interpreting swells of any kind.", "why": "The trainees succeeded only near the islands they had trained around and were at chance near unfamiliar ones, so the guarded inference is that the skill is island-specific rather than a general swell-reading ability."}, {"text": "allows them to interpret swell patterns near unfamiliar islands about as accurately as they do near familiar ones.", "why": "This is directly contradicted by the finding that the trainees performed “no better than chance” near unfamiliar islands.", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-inf-a2", "domain": "information", "skill": "inferences", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "After a rat runs through a maze, the neurons in its hippocampus that fired in sequence to represent locations along the route fire again in the same order—though far more rapidly—while the rat rests. In one experiment, researchers used targeted stimulation to disrupt this “replay” during the rest periods of one group of rats but left it intact in another group. Both groups had run the maze the same number of times. When later returned to the maze, the rats whose replay had been disrupted navigated it more slowly and made more wrong turns than the other rats did. This finding suggests that hippocampal replay during rest"}, "stem": "Which choice most logically completes the text?", "choices": [{"text": "may help strengthen the memory of a route rather than merely reproduce a record of it.", "why": "Disrupting replay impaired later navigation even though both groups had identical running experience, so the guarded inference is that replay actively contributes to consolidating the memory, not just passively reflecting it."}, {"text": "occurs only in animals that have recently completed a navigational task.", "why": "The experiment observed replay after maze running but tested no other conditions, so it cannot establish that replay happens “only” in that circumstance.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "prevents rats from forming any lasting memory of a route they have traveled.", "why": "Disrupting replay worsened performance rather than replay itself preventing memory, and the disrupted rats still navigated the maze, so “any lasting memory” is contradicted.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "causes the same neurons to fire during rest that had fired while the rat was running.", "why": "This merely restates the setup described in the first sentence; it reports a premise rather than drawing the conclusion the experiment supports.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}], "answer": 0},
{"id": "rw-inf-a3", "domain": "information", "skill": "inferences", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Traditional Mesoamerican cooks process maize by simmering and soaking it in an alkaline solution of limewater before grinding it, a technique called nixtamalization. Nutritionists have established that this process frees niacin, a vitamin that is chemically bound and nutritionally unavailable in untreated maize; historically, communities that relied on untreated maize as a staple often developed the niacin-deficiency disease pellagra, whereas communities that nixtamalized their maize did not. A more recent analysis has found that the alkaline treatment also degrades certain toxins produced by molds that grow on stored maize. Researchers therefore propose that the health benefits of nixtamalization for maize-dependent communities"}, "stem": "Which choice most logically completes the text?", "choices": [{"text": "arise chiefly from its ability to reduce exposure to mold-produced toxins rather than from any effect on niacin.", "why": "The historical evidence about pellagra centers on niacin; the text gives no basis for making toxin reduction the chief benefit, so this overstates the newer finding and reverses the emphasis.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "would vanish if those communities began to eat a wider variety of foods alongside maize.", "why": "The passage says nothing about dietary diversification or how it would affect nixtamalization's benefits; this introduces a scenario outside the premises.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "consist entirely in making the maize's bound niacin available to the people who eat it.", "why": "The word “entirely” ignores the newly reported degradation of mold toxins; it restates only the first benefit while excluding the second.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "may extend beyond improving the availability of niacin in the diet.", "why": "Because the alkaline process both frees niacin and degrades mold toxins, the guarded conclusion consistent with all the premises is that the benefits may reach beyond niacin availability."}], "answer": 3},
{"id": "rw-inf-a4", "domain": "information", "skill": "inferences", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Neuroscientists compared the brains of professional musicians and of people with no musical training. In the musicians, the area of the auditory cortex that responded to piano tones was larger than in the nonmusicians, and among the musicians its size was correlated with the age at which training had begun: those who had started earliest had the largest such areas. Notably, however, even musicians who had taken up their instruments as adults had larger response areas than the nonmusicians did. These results suggest that"}, "stem": "Which choice most logically completes the text?", "choices": [{"text": "musical training enlarges this region of the auditory cortex only when the training begins in childhood.", "why": "The word “only” is contradicted by the finding that adult-starting musicians still had larger response areas than nonmusicians.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "beginning musical training in childhood may enlarge this region more than beginning in adulthood does, though training begun in adulthood can enlarge it as well.", "why": "Earlier starts correlated with larger areas (a childhood advantage), yet adult-starters still exceeded nonmusicians (adult training still works), so this hedged two-part statement is the only one consistent with both findings."}, {"text": "the size of this region of the auditory cortex determines how accomplished a musician a person will become.", "why": "The study measured cortical size and training onset, not musical skill or achievement, so a claim about determining accomplishment is unsupported.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "adults who begin musical training show no change in this region of the auditory cortex over time.", "why": "The comparison is between groups at one time, not a tracking of individuals over time, and adult musicians actually had larger areas than nonmusicians, so this is unsupported.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-inf-a5", "domain": "information", "skill": "inferences", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Scattered across the Amazon basin are patches of unusually dark, fertile soil known as terra preta, which contain charcoal, broken pottery, and decomposed organic waste and are found at the sites of long-inhabited pre-Columbian settlements. Whereas the region's typical tropical soils lose their nutrients quickly, terra preta stays fertile for centuries; some patches remain productive today, long after the settlements that formed them were abandoned. Soil scientists have shown that the charcoal in terra preta both harbors dense communities of microbes and binds nutrients chemically, slowing the rate at which they wash away. From these findings, researchers conclude that terra preta's lasting fertility"}, "stem": "Which choice most logically completes the text?", "choices": [{"text": "cannot be reproduced in the agricultural soils used by farmers today.", "why": "The passage offers no evidence about attempts to reproduce terra preta in modern soils, so a claim about what “cannot” be done has no support.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "depends on the continued addition of organic waste by people living nearby.", "why": "This is contradicted by the statement that some patches remain productive long after their settlements were abandoned.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "makes it more fertile than any other kind of soil found anywhere in tropical regions.", "why": "The text compares terra preta only with the region's typical soils, not with every soil in all tropical regions, so “any other kind… anywhere” overreaches.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "is likely maintained at least partly by the properties of the charcoal it contains rather than by ongoing human activity.", "why": "Because patches stay fertile centuries after abandonment (ruling out ongoing human input) and the charcoal binds nutrients, the hedged inference is that the charcoal's properties help sustain fertility."}], "answer": 3},
{"id": "rw-inf-a6", "domain": "information", "skill": "inferences", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Researchers tracked a group of older adults—some lifelong bilinguals and some lifelong monolinguals—until those who developed Alzheimer's disease were diagnosed. On average, the bilinguals began showing the outward symptoms of the disease about four years later than the monolinguals did. Yet brain scans taken at the time of diagnosis revealed that the bilinguals' brains showed more extensive neurodegeneration than the monolinguals' brains did. On the basis of these findings, the researchers suggest that lifelong bilingualism"}, "stem": "Which choice most logically completes the text?", "choices": [{"text": "may allow people to sustain everyday cognitive functioning despite the brain damage the disease causes, rather than slowing that damage itself.", "why": "The bilinguals stayed symptom-free longer even though their brains showed more degeneration, so the guarded inference is that bilingualism buffers functioning rather than slowing the underlying pathology."}, {"text": "slows the physical progression of the brain damage caused by Alzheimer's disease.", "why": "This is contradicted by the scans showing that bilinguals had more extensive neurodegeneration at diagnosis, not less.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "protects people who speak two languages from ever developing Alzheimer's disease.", "why": "The bilinguals in the study did develop the disease, so the absolute claim that they are protected from “ever” developing it is contradicted.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "leads to a diagnosis of Alzheimer's disease at a younger age than monolingualism does.", "why": "Because bilinguals showed symptoms about four years later, they would be diagnosed at an older age, not a younger one.", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 0},
{"id": "rw-inf-c1", "domain": "information", "skill": "inferences", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Self-healing polymers repair damage when their chains diffuse across a cut surface and re-form reversible hydrogen bonds—a process that requires the chains to remain mobile at the healing temperature. Materials scientist Priya Raghunathan and colleagues synthesized a series of elastomers with progressively higher densities of hydrogen-bonding sites, expecting healing efficiency to rise in step. Instead, the most densely bonded variants recovered the least strength after being cut. The researchers observed that these variants were also markedly stiffer: abundant hydrogen bonds act as temporary crosslinks that restrict chain motion. The results therefore suggest that ______"}, "stem": "Which choice most logically completes the text?", "choices": [{"text": "the self-healing of Raghunathan's elastomers depends on a mechanism other than the re-formation of hydrogen bonds.", "why": "The passage states that healing works precisely by re-formation of reversible hydrogen bonds; the anomaly concerns bond density, not the mechanism itself.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "elastomers with relatively few hydrogen-bonding sites will recover their original strength completely, no matter how severely they are damaged.", "why": "The low-density variants merely healed better than the high-density ones; nothing supports the absolute claim of complete recovery regardless of damage severity.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "an abundance of hydrogen-bonding sites can undermine healing if the resulting temporary crosslinks leave polymer chains too immobile to diffuse across damaged surfaces.", "why": "Only this choice reconciles all premises: healing requires chain mobility, dense hydrogen bonds act as crosslinks that restrict that mobility, and the densest variants healed worst. The hedged 'can undermine' matches the evidence."}, {"text": "an elastomer's stiffness is a more accurate predictor of its healing efficiency than the density of its hydrogen-bonding sites is.", "why": "Stiffness is presented as a symptom of restricted chain motion, not as a competing predictor; the text never compares the predictive power of stiffness and bond density.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-inf-c2", "domain": "information", "skill": "inferences", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "The anonymous fourteenth-century poem Saint Erkenwald survives in a single manuscript copied decades after the poem's likely composition, and scholars have long assigned the poet to Cheshire on the basis of the manuscript's northwest Midlands dialect forms. Medieval scribes, however, routinely converted the spellings of the texts they copied into their own regional dialects, and philologist Marion Tredwell has documented that the Erkenwald scribe copied at least two other poems—each independently localized to a different part of England—using the same northwest Midlands forms throughout. If Tredwell's account of the scribe's habits is correct, then ______"}, "stem": "Which choice most logically completes the text?", "choices": [{"text": "the dialect forms of the Erkenwald manuscript may indicate where the scribe was trained rather than where the poet lived.", "why": "The scribe imposed the same northwest Midlands forms on poems from different regions, so those forms track the scribe's own dialect; the manuscript's dialect therefore points to the scribe, not necessarily the poet. The hedged 'may indicate' fits what the premises license."}, {"text": "the Erkenwald poet cannot have come from Cheshire, since the poem's dialect forms were introduced by the scribe.", "why": "Tredwell's evidence undermines the argument for Cheshire but cannot rule Cheshire out—the poet's own dialect could happen to match the scribe's.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "the two other poems copied by the scribe were most likely composed by the Erkenwald poet as well.", "why": "The other poems are described as independently localized to different regions; nothing in the passage suggests they share an author with Erkenwald.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "dialect evidence cannot help scholars determine the regional origin of any poem that survives in only one manuscript copy.", "why": "The passage documents one scribe's habits; it cannot support an absolute claim about dialect evidence for every single-manuscript poem.", "tag": "too-broad"}], "answer": 0},
{"id": "rw-inf-c3", "domain": "information", "skill": "inferences", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Metallic glasses—alloys cooled so rapidly that their atoms never settle into a crystal lattice—can be far stronger than conventional metals, but they typically fail catastrophically: deformation concentrates in narrow shear bands that propagate unimpeded through the uniform glassy structure. Engineers therefore embed soft crystalline particles in the glassy matrix, since a propagating shear band halts when it encounters a crystalline region. In a composite studied in Chen Wei's laboratory, however, the particles, though plentiful, were spaced farther apart than the distance a shear band travels before becoming a self-propagating crack that particles can no longer stop. Wei's team could therefore predict that the composite would ______"}, "stem": "Which choice most logically completes the text?", "choices": [{"text": "fail catastrophically at lower applied stresses than a particle-free metallic glass would.", "why": "The premises predict that the particles will fail to stop mature shear bands, not that they make the composite weaker than a particle-free glass; the text makes no comparison of failure stresses.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "arrest propagating shear bands effectively, given that the crystalline particles in the composite were plentiful.", "why": "The passage makes spacing, not abundance, decisive: because the spacing exceeds the distance at which a band becomes unstoppable, arrest should fail even though particles are plentiful.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "be stronger than a conventional crystalline metal while also proving tougher than a typical metallic glass.", "why": "The strength claim borrows a general premise about metallic glasses, but the toughness half is defeated: with particles spaced too widely, the arresting mechanism cannot operate.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "remain prone to catastrophic failure, since a shear band could grow into an unstoppable crack before reaching a crystalline particle.", "why": "This is the only prediction consistent with all premises: shear bands cause catastrophic failure, particles stop them only if encountered in time, and here the particle spacing exceeds the critical propagation distance."}], "answer": 3},
{"id": "rw-inf-c4", "domain": "information", "skill": "inferences", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Emily Dickinson's manuscripts are studded with dashes that her early editors replaced with conventional punctuation, and some critics have argued that the marks constitute a deliberate prosodic notation—a system for scoring pause and emphasis that regularized print editions erase. Examining Dickinson's surviving papers, literary historian Elena Vasquez-Moro finds that visually identical marks pervade the poet's household documents, including recipe transcriptions and drafts of routine correspondence, where a system for scoring poetic rhythm would serve no purpose. Vasquez-Moro does not deny that dashes in the poems may function expressively, but she cautions that ______"}, "stem": "Which choice most logically completes the text?", "choices": [{"text": "the dashes in Dickinson's poems cannot be expressive, since marks identical to them appear throughout her household documents.", "why": "Vasquez-Moro explicitly does not deny that the poems' dashes may function expressively; this choice converts her caution into the absolute denial she disavows.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "the appearance of a dash in one of Dickinson's poems is not by itself evidence that the poet intended the mark as prosodic notation.", "why": "Because identical marks appear where prosodic scoring would serve no purpose, a dash's mere presence cannot certify prosodic intent—exactly the limited, hedged caution the premises support while leaving expressive function possible."}, {"text": "print editions of Dickinson's poetry should reproduce the dashes exactly as they appear in her manuscripts.", "why": "Faithful reproduction is a genuine editorial position, but the passage's logic concerns what the marks prove about intention, not how editions should print them.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "critics who analyze Dickinson's punctuation should give the same weight to her household documents as to her poems.", "why": "The household documents serve as comparative evidence about the marks; nothing suggests critics should treat them as objects of equal interpretive attention.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-inf-c5", "domain": "information", "skill": "inferences", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Perovskite solar cells degrade rapidly when atmospheric moisture infiltrates and decomposes the light-absorbing perovskite layer, so manufacturers seal the cells behind moisture-impermeable barriers. Engineer Tomasz Krawiec and colleagues subjected encapsulated cells to an accelerated-aging test combining high humidity with a temperature of 85 degrees Celsius. The barriers held—post-test analysis found no trace of moisture inside the cells—yet the cells still lost nearly a third of their efficiency. The team also found that iodide ions had migrated out of the perovskite layer and into an adjacent electrode, a process known to accelerate at elevated temperatures. Krawiec and colleagues concluded that ______"}, "stem": "Which choice most logically completes the text?", "choices": [{"text": "keeping moisture out of perovskite cells may be insufficient to preserve their efficiency, because elevated temperatures can drive a degradation process that encapsulation does not block.", "why": "This is the only conclusion consistent with every finding: the barriers held, no moisture entered, efficiency still fell, and a known heat-accelerated process (iodide migration) was observed. The hedge 'may be insufficient' matches evidence from a single test."}, {"text": "the cells' efficiency losses were most likely caused by trace amounts of moisture too small for the team's post-test analysis to detect.", "why": "This speculation runs against the stated post-test analysis and ignores the observed iodide migration, which supplies a moisture-free explanation for the losses.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "iodide ions damage perovskite cells only when the cells' interiors are simultaneously exposed to high humidity.", "why": "The cells' interiors were moisture-free, yet migration and efficiency loss still occurred—evidence that heat alone can drive the damage, the opposite of requiring internal humidity.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "encapsulation barriers accelerate the migration of iodide ions by trapping heat inside the cells.", "why": "No premise indicates that barriers trap heat; the test itself imposed the 85-degree temperature, and migration is said to accelerate with temperature generally.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 0},
{"id": "rw-inf-c6", "domain": "information", "skill": "inferences", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "In the mid-twentieth century, some scholars treated the density of formulas—repeated metrical phrases—in Old English verse as a test of a poem's origins: verse built largely from formulas was presumed to have been composed orally, in performance, rather than in writing. The Old English Metrical Psalms, however, is a close translation of a Latin source and thus must have been composed by a literate poet working from a written text. Analyzing the translation, philologist Ingrid Halvorsen found that its formulaic density matches or exceeds that of poems long classified as oral compositions. Halvorsen's finding suggests that ______"}, "stem": "Which choice most logically completes the text?", "choices": [{"text": "the Old English poems long classified as oral compositions were probably, like the Metrical Psalms, produced by literate poets working from written sources.", "why": "One demonstrably written poem with high formulaic density discredits the test; it does not show that other poems were also written—their origins simply remain undetermined.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "translations of Latin texts into Old English generally exhibit more formulas than original Old English poems do.", "why": "The finding concerns a single translation, and 'matches or exceeds' cannot establish a general pattern about translations as a class.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "the Metrical Psalms was composed orally in performance, notwithstanding its dependence on a written Latin source.", "why": "The passage establishes that a close translation of a written Latin source must have been composed in writing; this choice contradicts that premise.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "a high density of formulas cannot by itself establish that an Old English poem was composed orally.", "why": "Halvorsen found a poem that must have been composed in writing yet is as formulaic as putatively oral poems, so formulaic density alone cannot discriminate oral from written composition—the only conclusion all premises support."}], "answer": 3},
{"id": "rw-inf-b1", "domain": "information", "skill": "inferences", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Lipid residues absorbed into the walls of unglazed cooking pots can survive for millennia, and their molecular signatures reliably distinguish dairy fats from the fats of butchered animals. At Karayün Höyük, a seventh-millennium-BCE settlement in central Anatolia, archaeologist Demet Aksoy's team found unmistakable dairy signatures in nearly half of the vessels sampled. Yet the site's animal-bone assemblage is dominated by wild game, and its few domesticated animals show the mortality profile of herds slaughtered young for meat, not kept alive for milking. Since absorbed residues record what a pot actually held, Aksoy cautiously concludes that ______"}, "stem": "Which choice most logically completes the text?", "choices": [{"text": "the inhabitants of Karayün Höyük maintained substantial milking herds whose remains simply have not yet been recovered in excavations.", "why": "The passage forecloses this: the recovered bone assemblage shows a meat-oriented mortality profile, and this choice must posit unexcavated evidence that contradicts the record rather than a conclusion that follows from it.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "the settlement's few domesticated animals were slaughtered young for meat rather than kept alive for milking.", "why": "This merely restates a premise the passage has already given about the mortality profile; it does nothing to resolve the tension between the dairy residues and the absence of milking herds.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "milk or milk products were likely arriving at the settlement from outside—perhaps through exchange with herding communities—rather than being supplied by its own animals.", "why": "Only this reconciles all the premises: the pots demonstrably held dairy ('residues record what a pot actually held'), yet the fauna show no milking economy, so the milk was likely obtained from elsewhere; the hedged phrasing matches Aksoy's 'cautiously concludes.'"}, {"text": "the lipid-analysis technique cannot dependably separate dairy fats from carcass fats in pots that were used to prepare many kinds of food.", "why": "This contradicts a stated premise: the passage says the molecular signatures 'reliably distinguish' dairy fats from the fats of butchered animals, so doubting the method reverses rather than completes the text's logic.", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-inf-b2", "domain": "information", "skill": "inferences", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Because speakers adapt a borrowed word to their own language's sound system at the moment of transfer, loanwords preserve the donor language's pronunciation as it existed at that time. Hungarian contains hundreds of words borrowed from neighboring Slavic dialects. Some, such as galamb (\"dove,\" from Slavic *golǫbь), retain traces of nasal vowels that had disappeared from those dialects by roughly the end of the tenth century; others whose Slavic source words also contained nasal vowels show no trace of them. Historical linguists therefore infer that ______"}, "stem": "Which choice most logically completes the text?", "choices": [{"text": "Hungarian's borrowing from Slavic was not a single episode: words retaining nasal traces likely entered the language before the vowels were lost, while those lacking such traces may have been acquired later.", "why": "The fossilization principle plus the split evidence—some loans preserve nasal vowels lost 'by roughly the end of the tenth century,' while others from nasal-bearing sources lack them—supports exactly this two-stage, suitably hedged conclusion."}, {"text": "all of the Slavic loanwords in Hungarian had entered the language by the end of the tenth century.", "why": "Too absolute: the loans whose Slavic sources had nasal vowels but which show no trace of them point to borrowing after the vowels were lost, so not every loan can predate the tenth century.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "the Slavic dialects neighboring Hungarian must have retained nasal vowels for several centuries longer than scholars have supposed.", "why": "Nothing supports redating the sound change; the passage treats the tenth-century loss as established, and the two strata of loanwords are fully explained without revising it.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "the loanwords lacking nasal traces entered Hungarian earlier than the ones that retain them.", "why": "This reverses the fossilization logic: words preserving the nasal vowels reflect the older Slavic pronunciation and are therefore the earlier borrowings, not the later ones.", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 0}
);
